  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nRidging aloft will maintain the hazy, very warm, and dry \nconditions for at least the next couple of days. Shortwave energy \nand instability capable of initiating shower and thunderstorm \nactivity looks to remain north of the Canadian border during this \nperiod, though some isolated storms may begin to creep towards the\nHi-Line Tuesday evening and night. Seasonably breezy conditions \nwill continue with wind gusts exceeding 20 mph at times, \nespecially this afternoon and evening. \n\nA Pacific trough begins to move southeastward through BC/AB and \ninto the Northern Rockies sometime Wednesday through Friday. \nEnsembles are coming into better agreement with temperatures \nfalling below average Thursday and Friday, but still differ on how\nwidespread the rainfall will be. Shower and thunderstorm activity\nlooks to increase from north to south with areas along the Hi-\nLine seeing scattered showers and storms as early as Tuesday \nevening. The Wednesday night through Thursday time period is \ngenerally favored to see the most widespread rainfall. While some \nareas may see beneficial rains and cooling temperatures, the \nconvective nature of this activity may also bring some strong to \nsevere storms with localized strong gusty winds, hail, and heavy \ndownpours capable of flooding of normally flood prone areas. ML \nCAPE values look to generally run in the 300 to 800 j/kg range \nwith the highest values gradually moving south into central and \nsouthwest Montana by late Thursday into Friday.\n\nThe ridging aloft begins to build back in heading into next \nweekend, but another trough approaching the Pacific NW coast will \nbring southwesterly flow aloft within striking distance of the \nNorthern Rockies. This will result in temperatures warming back \nup, along with daily scattered shower and thunderstorm activity. \n- RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nBreezy conditions, shifting winds, and localized elevated fire \nconcerns...\n\nThe combination of very warm, dry, and breezy conditions will \nresult in localized fire weather conditions for the next couple of\ndays. Surface boundaries and or diurnal mixing will bring wind \nshifts and gusts in excess of 20 mph at times. Afternoon humidity \nwill fall to or near critically low levels with only moderate to \npoor overnight recoveries, driest over southwest Montana. Those \nworking to contain ongoing fires should continue be mindful of the\ndry conditions and breezy, shifting winds.\n\nCooler temperatures, some stronger storms, and beneficial rains \nWednesday through Friday...\n\nEnsemble guidance is now coming into agreement with cooler and \nwetter conditions moving in late Wednesday through Friday. Climate\nanomaly indices, including the ECMWF Extreme Forecast Index are \nshowing signals for precipitation amounts above climatology for \nthis time of year. While this rainfall is definitely welcome, its \nconvective nature can result in uneven distribution and increased \ninstances of stronger storms capable of localized strong wind \ngusts, hail, and even flooding of normally prone areas. At this \ntime, the most widespread precipitation looks to occur on \nThursday. \n\nScattered gusty thunderstorms this weekend...\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft approaching the Northern Rockies will \nmaintain mid-level moisture and shortwave energy this weekend \nwhile the surface boundary layer warms up and dries out. If this \npattern comes to fruition, there will be scattered showers and \nthunderstorms for both days, mostly in the afternoon and evening. \nThe primary hazards will be localized strong, gusty winds, and \nlightning. - RCG",
